<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net">
  <diagram name="Bridge Pattern" id="bridge">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1434" dy="844" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1400">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        
        <!-- Title -->
        <mxCell id="title" value="BRIDGE DESIGN PATTERN - Decouple Abstraction from Implementation" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=20;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- DEFINITION -->
        <mxCell id="def_title" value="DEFINITION: Decouples an Abstraction from its Implementation so they can vary INDEPENDENTLY" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="70" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <mxCell id="problem_title" value="THE PROBLEM: Tightly Coupled Abstraction &amp; Implementation" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="120" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="problem" value="ORIGINAL DESIGN (WITHOUT BRIDGE):&#xa;&#xa;abstract class LivingThing {&#xa;  abstract breatheProcess();  // Tightly coupled!&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class Dog extends LivingThing {&#xa;  breatheProcess() {&#xa;    // Breathe through NOSE&#xa;    // Inhale oxygen, exhale CO2&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class Fish extends LivingThing {&#xa;  breatheProcess() {&#xa;    // Breathe through GILLS&#xa;    // Absorb oxygen from water&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class Tree extends LivingThing {&#xa;  breatheProcess() {&#xa;    // Breathe through LEAVES&#xa;    // Inhale CO2, exhale O2&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;THE PROBLEM:&#xa;&#xa;Want to add NEW breathing process:&#xa;  'BirdBreathing' (inhale through NARES, exhale through mouth)&#xa;&#xa;✗ CAN'T add new breathing process alone!&#xa;✗ MUST create new child class (Bird extends LivingThing)&#xa;✗ Breathing implementation TIGHTLY COUPLED to LivingThing hierarchy&#xa;✗ Can't add breathing process independently&#xa;✗ Can't reuse breathing processes across different animals&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WHAT IF:&#xa;  Dog wants to use TreeBreathe? (impossible!)&#xa;  New animal wants LandBreathe? (must create new class!)&#xa;  Want to change breathing at runtime? (impossible!)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;ROOT CAUSE:&#xa;  Abstraction (LivingThing hierarchy) and&#xa;  Implementation (breathing process)&#xa;  are TIGHTLY COUPLED!&#xa;&#xa;  They CANNOT vary independently!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="160" width="740" height="680" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- SOLUTION -->
        <mxCell id="solution_title" value="THE SOLUTION: Bridge Pattern - Independent Hierarchies" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="120" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="solution" value="NEW DESIGN (WITH BRIDGE):&#xa;&#xa;// IMPLEMENTOR (breathing processes - can grow independently!)&#xa;interface BreatheImplementor {&#xa;  void breathe();&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class LandBreathe implements BreatheImplementor {&#xa;  void breathe() {&#xa;    System.out.println('Breathe through NOSE');&#xa;    System.out.println('Inhale O2, Exhale CO2');&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class WaterBreathe implements BreatheImplementor {&#xa;  void breathe() {&#xa;    System.out.println('Breathe through GILLS');&#xa;    System.out.println('Absorb O2 from water');&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class TreeBreathe implements BreatheImplementor {&#xa;  void breathe() {&#xa;    System.out.println('Breathe through LEAVES');&#xa;    System.out.println('Inhale CO2, Exhale O2');&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class BirdBreathe implements BreatheImplementor {  // ✓ NEW! Added independently!&#xa;  void breathe() {&#xa;    System.out.println('Breathe through NARES');&#xa;    System.out.println('Inhale O2, Exhale CO2');&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// ABSTRACTION (animals - can grow independently!)&#xa;abstract class LivingThing {&#xa;  protected BreatheImplementor breatheImplementor;  // BRIDGE!&#xa;  &#xa;  public LivingThing(BreatheImplementor implementor) {&#xa;    this.breatheImplementor = implementor;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  public void breatheProcess() {&#xa;    breatheImplementor.breathe();  // Delegate to implementor!&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class Dog extends LivingThing {&#xa;  public Dog(BreatheImplementor implementor) {&#xa;    super(implementor);  // Pass breathing strategy&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class Fish extends LivingThing {&#xa;  public Fish(BreatheImplementor implementor) {&#xa;    super(implementor);&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// CLIENT (can mix any abstraction + implementation!)&#xa;class Client {&#xa;  public static void main(String[] args) {&#xa;    // Dog with land breathing&#xa;    LivingThing dog = new Dog(new LandBreathe());&#xa;    dog.breatheProcess();  // Breathe through NOSE&#xa;    &#xa;    // Fish with water breathing&#xa;    LivingThing fish = new Fish(new WaterBreathe());&#xa;    fish.breatheProcess();  // Breathe through GILLS&#xa;    &#xa;    // Tree with tree breathing&#xa;    LivingThing tree = new Tree(new TreeBreathe());&#xa;    tree.breatheProcess();  // Breathe through LEAVES&#xa;    &#xa;    // ✓ Dog with TREE breathing (now possible!)&#xa;    LivingThing mutantDog = new Dog(new TreeBreathe());&#xa;    mutantDog.breatheProcess();  // Breathe through LEAVES!&#xa;    &#xa;    // ✓ Change breathing at runtime!&#xa;    tree.breatheImplementor = new WaterBreathe();&#xa;    tree.breatheProcess();  // Now breathes through GILLS!&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;BENEFITS:&#xa;  ✓ Abstraction &amp; Implementation vary INDEPENDENTLY&#xa;  ✓ Add new breathing process WITHOUT new animal class&#xa;  ✓ Add new animal WITHOUT new breathing process&#xa;  ✓ Mix &amp; match any combination at runtime&#xa;  ✓ Reuse breathing processes across animals&#xa;  ✓ Change breathing strategy dynamically" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="160" width="740" height="1080" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- BRIDGE VS STRATEGY -->
        <mxCell id="vs_title" value="BRIDGE vs STRATEGY (Same UML, Different Intent!)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="880" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="vs_strategy" value="CONFUSION: UML looks IDENTICAL to Strategy Pattern!&#xa;&#xa;┌─────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐&#xa;│  ASPECT         │  BRIDGE PATTERN              │  STRATEGY PATTERN            │&#xa;├─────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤&#xa;│  INTENT         │  DECOUPLE abstraction from   │  CHANGE BEHAVIOR of object   │&#xa;│                 │  implementation so BOTH can  │  dynamically at RUNTIME      │&#xa;│                 │  VARY INDEPENDENTLY          │                              │&#xa;│                 │                              │                              │&#xa;│  FOCUS          │  SEPARATION OF CONCERNS      │  RUNTIME FLEXIBILITY         │&#xa;│                 │  (split logic into layers)  │  (swap algorithm on-the-fly) │&#xa;│                 │                              │                              │&#xa;│  PROBLEM        │  Implementation tightly      │  Want to change object       │&#xa;│                 │  coupled to abstraction.     │  behavior without subclasses │&#xa;│                 │  Can't grow independently.   │                              │&#xa;│                 │                              │                              │&#xa;│  GOAL           │  Both hierarchies GROW       │  Client CHOOSES algorithm    │&#xa;│                 │  independently (new animals, │  at runtime (swap strategies)│&#xa;│                 │  new breathing processes)    │                              │&#xa;│                 │                              │                              │&#xa;│  EXAMPLE        │  LivingThing + BreatheImpl   │  PaymentContext + Strategy   │&#xa;│                 │  Dog can use ANY breathing   │  Cart can use ANY payment    │&#xa;│                 │  Fish can use ANY breathing  │  method (CreditCard, PayPal) │&#xa;│                 │                              │                              │&#xa;│  USAGE          │  Add new animal WITHOUT      │  Change payment method for   │&#xa;│                 │  adding breathing process    │  SAME cart at runtime        │&#xa;│                 │  Add new breathing WITHOUT   │                              │&#xa;│                 │  adding animal               │                              │&#xa;│                 │                              │                              │&#xa;│  CLIENT CODE    │  LivingThing dog =           │  Context ctx = new Context(  │&#xa;│                 │    new Dog(landBreathe);     │      new CreditCardStrategy);│&#xa;│                 │  dog.breatheProcess();       │  ctx.execute();              │&#xa;│                 │                              │  ctx.setStrategy(paypal);    │&#xa;│                 │  // Later add BirdBreathe    │  ctx.execute();  // Changed! │&#xa;│                 │  // No Dog code changes!     │                              │&#xa;└─────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY DIFFERENCE:&#xa;&#xa;BRIDGE:&#xa;  'I'm DESIGNING from the start so abstraction &amp; implementation can GROW separately'&#xa;  Design-time concern: Separate logic into independent hierarchies&#xa;  Example: 10 animals × 5 breathing processes = 50 combinations WITHOUT 50 classes!&#xa;&#xa;STRATEGY:&#xa;  'I want to SWAP algorithm for SAME object at runtime'&#xa;  Runtime concern: Change behavior dynamically&#xa;  Example: Same shopping cart, different payment methods at checkout&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;SIMPLE RULE:&#xa;  Both can grow? Need separation of concerns?        → BRIDGE&#xa;  Want to change behavior at runtime for same object? → STRATEGY" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="920" width="1520" height="700" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <mxCell id="q_title" value="INTERVIEW QUESTIONS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1660" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="questions" value="Q1: When to use Bridge Pattern?&#xa;A1: Use when: (1) Abstraction AND implementation both need to extend independently, (2) Want to avoid permanent binding between abstraction/implementation, (3) Changes in implementation shouldn't affect client code, (4) Want to share implementation among multiple objects, (5) Combinatorial explosion (N abstractions × M implementations = N×M classes). Example: GUI frameworks (Window abstraction × OS implementation), Database drivers (API abstraction × DB implementation).&#xa;&#xa;Q2: Bridge Pattern real-world example in Java/JDBC?&#xa;A2: JDBC is perfect example! DriverManager (abstraction) + JDBC Drivers (implementation). Code: Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url). Client uses Connection interface (abstraction), actual driver (MySQL, Oracle, Postgres) is implementation. Can swap databases WITHOUT changing code. Add new DB? Just new driver (implementation). Change SQL API? Just abstraction changes. They grow independently!&#xa;&#xa;Q3: How is Bridge different from Adapter?&#xa;A3: INTENT! Adapter makes INCOMPATIBLE interfaces work together (after design). Bridge separates abstraction from implementation (during design). Adapter is REACTIVE (fix existing incompatibility). Bridge is PROACTIVE (prevent future coupling). Example: Adapter wraps legacy API to match new interface. Bridge designs from start so both sides can evolve. Adapter = retrofit, Bridge = foresight.&#xa;&#xa;Q4: Performance overhead of Bridge Pattern?&#xa;A4: Minimal. One extra indirection (abstraction → implementor reference → concrete implementation). Modern JVMs optimize method calls well. GAINS outweigh cost: (1) Avoid class explosion (N×M combinations), (2) Runtime flexibility (swap implementations), (3) Easier testing (mock implementors), (4) Maintainability (change one hierarchy independently). Trade: tiny latency vs massive code reduction &amp; flexibility.&#xa;&#xa;Q5: Can Bridge and Strategy be used together?&#xa;A5: YES! They serve different purposes. Bridge separates STRUCTURE (abstraction from implementation). Strategy changes BEHAVIOR (algorithm selection). Example: Payment system with Bridge (PaymentAPI abstraction × Gateway implementation) + Strategy (fraud detection algorithms). Bridge handles 'what gateway', Strategy handles 'how to validate'. Complementary, not competing!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1710" width="1520" height="480" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
